<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> SCP-XXXX is a humanoid creature with smooth black skin and no features except for its mouth. You can only see its reflection, although it does not work when the reflection is recorded. As such, an image cannot be provided unless it is an illustration of the specimen.</p>
<p>It can phase through everything that isn't living flesh (e.g. LIVING animals, LIVING humans), but this ability is nullified if its temperature is lowered below -50 degrees Celsius, or 223 degrees Kelvin.</p>
<p>Once in containment, SCP-XXXX does not require any sort of furniture in its containment cell (made of mirror-like reflective metal to ensure its presence), only needing a cooling system to keep it from phasing out of the room. It does need to be fed, and its meals consist of wild animals and, if a test takes an unfortunate turn, an unlucky Class-D personnel.</p>
<p>It acts like an aggressive and predatory animal despite its humanoid form, and will attack anything, including other SCPs. It will claw at the walls of its cell, and if it breaks the cooling system, it can breach containment. This aggression is seemingly nullified while SCP-XXXX is in the presence of SCP-XXXX-B.</p>
<p>SCP-XXXX-B is the infant born from SCP-XXXX. It resembles a humanoid infant with lighter colored skin than its parent. It shares the same invisibility as its parent, but has not yet displayed any signs of aggression.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The details of SCP-XXXX's containment are yet undisclosed, but many researchers in the Foundation have suggested that it be reclassified to Thaumiel due to its aforementioned aggression. While the subject is still up for debate, its classification remains Keter, due to its extreme aggression and repeated attempts at breaching containment.</p>
<p>At some point, some people suggested that SCP-XXXX could be the last piece in the puzzle that is the destruction of SCP-682, but due to the incredibly high likelihood of failure, the possibility has been dropped completely.</p>
<p>In the months following the containment of SCP-XXXX, it is discovered to have been pregnant, and it gives birth to SCP-XXXX-B while in containment. This, followed by repeated sightings of SCP-XXXX out in the wild despite it being in containment confirms that SCP-XXXX is not an isolated anomaly, but an entire species of creatures with the same abilities.</p>

							</div>

								
					
